																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13970941/seeking-greenlight-for-scp-idea">Seeking Greenlight for SCP Idea</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4863092">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> This SCP is a normal human male who is nothing out of the ordinary. What makes this SCP anomalous is the fact that everyone somehow finds him incredibly interesting. SCP researchers become obsessed with studying him and are endlessly fascinated with the quite normal test data he provides.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> The narrative would begin with researchers studying basic aspects of the SCP (height, weight, blood pressure, ect.). After getting these numbers (which are all average for an adult male), the researchers become endlessly fascinated with the man and eagerly perform more tests on the SCP. Over time, the tests grow in number as the researchers become more and more fascinated with his average body and mind. Every time a test is completed, the researchers look at the data, cannot believe what they are seeing, and come up with new tests. They continue to hook up machines and the tests grow in danger. Eventually, the man is killed by a test (maybe electrocution? maybe another SCP?). After his death, researchers no longer find the man interesting and fall into a depression as they are no longer able to test on the man.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> The article never acknowledges the fact that the data received is ordinary. The writer of the article writes as if the SCP is truly the most interesting man in the world. No one believes the man is ordinary. No one can believe he is ordinary.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> Not sure if this idea has been done before. Any advice helps. Thanks.</p>

